From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diego Garcia Brisa <d.garciabrisa@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 07:57:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] windows: serve CEF webviews from WebView2 when CEF is not
 bundled

An app that bundles CEF on Windows only for Chromium pages it drives over
the DevTools protocol ships libcef.dll and its resources, hundreds of
megabytes installed, and initializes CEF at every launch. Without CEF, a
renderer: "cef" webview fell back to an ordinary WebView2 webview that no
DevTools client can reach.

Such webviews now open in a WebView2 environment of their own, the
Chromium environment. It has its own user data folder
(<LOCALAPPDATA>\<identifier>\<channel>\WebView2Chromium), so its own
browser process, and only that process serves DevTools, on the port CEF
would have used: ELECTROBUN_CEF_REMOTE_DEBUGGING_PORT, then build.json's
chromiumFlags, then the dev-build scan (chromium_flags.h). The app's
system-webview windows stay in their environments, never on the port. It
gets no remote-allow-origins, so web pages cannot open a DevTools socket.

A browser process serves one port, so partitions are profiles of the one
environment rather than user data folders: persist:<name> is the on-disk
profile <name>, any other name an InPrivate profile of that name, shared
by its webviews and gone when the last one closes, and an empty partition
an InPrivate profile of its own. Names that are not short lowercase ASCII
become a hash (webview2_chromium_environment.h, with a test). The
environment starts with the first such webview, not at launch.

The main environment, which serves only the app's own views, runs its GPU
and network service in its browser process (--in-process-gpu,
NetworkServiceInProcess2): each was a helper process of about 40-45 MB.
The Chromium environment, which loads arbitrary sites, keeps them apart.
